# Known false positives for the `secrets-scan` CI job (gitleaks).
#
# Each entry is a gitleaks Fingerprint (<commit>:<file>:<rule-id>:<line>) --
# see https://github.com/gitleaks/gitleaks#gitleaksignore. gitleaks scans
# `git log -p`, i.e. each historical commit's own patch content, so a fixed
# string already committed cannot be un-flagged by editing it in a later
# commit (that only changes what the *new* commit's patch contains); this
# file is the supported way to allowlist a specific historical finding
# without rewriting git history.

# tests/test_openai_runner.py: a hardcoded OPENAI_API_KEY-shaped placeholder
# value in a unit test fixture (asserting the mocked OpenAI client is
# constructed with the settings-provided key), not a real credential. Its
# "sk-"-prefixed shape trips gitleaks' generic-api-key rule on
# entropy/prefix alone. NOTE: do not paste the actual placeholder string
# into this comment -- gitleaks scans this file's own content too, and a
# literal copy here would just create a second, self-inflicted finding
# (see the commit this exact mistake was fixed in).
3e0d6eec44789a789aa2c40ad63e27864e314031:tests/test_openai_runner.py:generic-api-key:743

# The first fix for the above (commit 9f4481f) made exactly the mistake
# this file's own comment now warns against: it quoted the placeholder
# value verbatim in this file's own explanatory comment, which created a
# second, self-inflicted finding in .gitleaksignore itself at that commit.
9f4481f32408a1a182661f06639418a5d9cffa7c:.gitleaksignore:generic-api-key:11
